<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  On the dashes below: this comment uses em-dashes where the rest of the repo
  writes a doubled hyphen. That is deliberate, and normalising it back to house
  style breaks the build in a way nothing in the gate catches. A doubled hyphen
  is illegal inside an XML comment, and while CFPropertyList is lenient enough
  that `plutil -lint` passes, AMFI's parser is not: pointing codesign at this
  file directly fails with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error" and leaves the
  binary unsigned. It is latent today only because Xcode's packaging step strips
  the comment before codesign reads the file. The literal codesign invocation
  for reading the entitlement back off a signed binary lives in
  apps/scanner/CMakeLists.txt, whose shell-style comments can carry the doubled
  hyphen this one cannot.

  A live 1 cm reconstruction is a genuinely large-memory app, and the default
  per-app jetsam limit is what killed it: at 3.2 M triangles the mesh arenas held
  3089 MB (resident_arena_bytes, summed over the mesh_slots ring) beside an
  805 MB voxel grid, and the OS sent SIGKILL — no OutOfMemory to report, the
  process simply disappears.

  increased-memory-limit raises that jetsam ceiling. Bridge/MemoryBudget reads
  the raised ceiling at runtime, which is the point of having it: no figure here
  needs to be trusted for the app to know its own position.

  Three different ceilings get confused with each other, so this records which
  is which. All three are quoted against the same base, the device's *nominal*
  16 GiB (16384 MiB) on this iPad Pro (M5, 11-inch):

    - hw.memsize reports 15469 MB. That is not a fourth number and not a
      contradiction: iOS reports installed RAM net of firmware and
      secure-enclave carve-outs, about 915 MB here. It is the least useful of
      these for sizing, because no process is offered all of it.
    - The jetsam ceiling is what increased-memory-limit moves and the only one
      of the three whose breach kills the process outright, with no Status and
      nothing in the log.
    - MoltenVK's "GPU memory available" banner reports 10922 MB, which is
      Metal's recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize, two thirds of 16384. An earlier
      version of this comment recorded that number as the jetsam ceiling; it is
      a different quantity and was never a process memory limit. It is also the
      *lowest* of the three, and it is the one the voxel grid and the mesh
      arenas are actually charged against, since both are Metal buffers.

  So do not read a comfortable jetsam percentage as headroom: the working set
  binds first, and the read-out prints both for that reason.

  Note what this does *not* establish. The jetsam ceiling and hw.memsize sit
  close together on a 16 GB device precisely because of the carve-out above, so
  their ratio is not evidence of anything, in either direction — an entitled and
  an unentitled build are not told apart by it, and on a phone build the ceiling
  sits below hw.memsize either way. The way to check the entitlement is to read
  it off the signed binary; the way to check the ceiling is Bridge/MemoryBudget.

  extended-virtual-addressing would normally sit beside it — the arena ring can
  exceed the 4 GB a 32-bit address space covers — but it is deliberately absent:
  a **personal** (free) development team cannot provision it. Xcode fails the
  build outright with "Personal development teams ... do not support the Extended
  Virtual Addressing capability", so adding it back costs the whole build, not
  just the capability. It needs a paid Apple Developer Program membership with
  the capability enabled on the App ID. increased-memory-limit alone does
  provision on a personal team.

  Not a licence to be careless: this moves the wall, it does not remove it.
  iOS still jetsams entitled processes under system-wide memory pressure, and
  the working set above binds below the jetsam ceiling regardless. voxel_size,
  FusionConfig::max_buckets and mesh_slots are still what decide whether a scan
  fits, and the occupancy guard in Fusion::fuse is still what keeps the *GPU*
  out of trouble independently of any of this.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
